   652	## <a id="combatencounterchecklist"></a>The Combat Encounter Checklist
   653	
   654	Sometimes all a game needs is an interesting location and some cool monsters to fight, setting up a combat encounter that a GM might build right at the table. The characters go somewhere, everyone decides it's time for a fun fight, and you whip something up. Or you determine that, given the circumstances going on in the story, it's time for the characters to run into some opposition, and you're off to the races.
   655	
   656	Sometimes we need more, though, particularly for big set-piece battles or boss fights. When it's time to build an interesting and dynamic encounter, the following checklist can help determine what options a big combat might need:
   657	
   658	* Interesting monsters
   659	* A fantastic location
   660	* Zone-wide effects
   661	* Traps and hazards
   662	* Advantageous positions
   663	* Interactive objects
   664	* Cover
   665	* Difficult or fantastic terrain
   666	* A goal
   667	
   668	No battle needs all these things, but it's worth running through the list to see which options fit the sort of combat scenario you're putting together.
   669	
   670	### Interesting Monsters
   671	
   672	For a big, self-contained combat encounter, a single monster usually won't do it. Even several monsters of the same type might not prove interesting enough for a big fight. Complex, climactic battles often work best with two or three creature types that work well together—big bruisers up front and powerful artillery in the back, for example.However, having more than three types of creatures in any one battle is going to be hard to manage. As such, designing a big set-piece battle is also a great time to think about waves of monsters.
   673	
   674	### A Fantastic Location
   675	
   676	An empty, 50-foot-square room doesn't lend itself to an interesting set-piece battle. We want fantastic rooms with interesting shapes, lots of room to move around, and a cool environment for the characters to spend time in. Great self-contained fights are like theme parks where the characters can climb up big statues, swing from chandeliers, and dance across elevated platforms. Whether you're playing online or in person, you can purchase battle maps showing off interesting locations, or might find maps that cartographers have released for free. Build a library of cool maps that inspire your players to enjoy the scenery while they're kicking ass.
   677	
   678	However, you want to ensure that your fantastic location isn't too big. It's no fun to have a character spend multiple rounds running to the far side of an arena—only to arrive just in time to watch the other characters drop the big bad to the mat. Let all the characters get to the meaty part of a location in two moves at most.
   679	
   680	### Zone-Wide Effects
   681	
   682	Sometimes a combat environment has a big ongoing effect—something that impacts all the creatures in the area, no matter where they are. Such zone-wide effects can make a fight more interesting, as with any of the following examples:
   683	
   684	* Unholy energy in a crypt makes healing magic only half as effective as normal.
   685	* Supernatural fire negates any creature's resistance to fire, and turns immunity to fire into resistance.
   686	* Psychic wailing forces each character to succeed on a DC 10 Constitution check to successfully cast a spell.
   687	* Periodic bolts of lightning strike, with each creature in combat having a 1-in-4 chance of being struck at the start of their turn.
   688	* An arcane rift causes each damaging spell cast in a fight to deal an extra 2d6 force damage.
   689	* An aura of bloody rage fills the area, granting each combatant advantage on attack rolls.
   690	* A rift to a realm of chaos causes all spells to trigger a wild magic surge.
   691	* The god of blood infuses all melee attacks with an extra die of damage.
   692	* A rift in space-time lets a creature swap places with an enemy within 60 feet if that enemy fails a DC 12 Wisdom saving throw.
   693	* A thick fog makes it impossible to see creatures more than 30 feet away.
   694	
   695	Avoid zone-wide effects that are just plain annoying. Having creatures fall down a lot because of icy floors sounds fun—until all the characters are lying on their backs and the players are wishing they'd never entered the fight in the first place.
   696	
   697	Likewise, certain effects hurt some classes more than others. Disadvantage on attack rolls hurts martial combatants more than spellcasters. Limiting movement hurts melee attackers, while limiting visibility hurts ranged attackers. Be aware of when a zone-wide effect affects some characters more than others, so that you can change it up if needed.
   698	
   699	### Traps and Hazards
   700	
   701	Certain parts of a battlefield might contain traps or hazards. Some of these might be easily seen, such as bladed pillars or spike-lined pits. Others might come as a surprise, such as a trap door over an acid pool.
   702	
   703	Characters with high passive Wisdom (Perception) scores might notice hidden traps automatically, or you might give each character a chance to make a Wisdom (Perception) check requiring no action—maybe even rolling on their behalf—to detect a trap before stumbling into it.
   704	
   705	Make sure these traps matter if you're going to put them in an encounter. Traps that are too far out of the way might never come into play. Likewise, it can be fun for players to spring traps on their opponents, so don't use them only as a threat against the characters.
   706	
   707	### Advantageous Positions
   708	
   709	Getting the characters to enter an arena (literal or metaphorical) and move around can be hard. Advantageous positions give them a reason to do so. Areas of high ground where they can gain cover against their foes—and perhaps advantage on attacks—are highly sought after by ranged attackers. Arcane circles that infuse a spellcaster's magic with greater power might draw wizards into a room. This approach can turn a whole encounter into a fun game of "king of the hill" as the characters and their enemies fight for superior position.
   710	
   711	### Interactive Objects
   712	
   713	Make sure that the battlefield features some interactive objects. This can include any physical features the characters can manipulate and use to their advantage in a fight, including things like the following:
   714	
   715	* Crumbling statues that can be easily toppled
   716	* Pillars that collapse part of the ceiling
   717	* Chandeliers upon which to swing
   718	* Ballistas the characters can use to fire upon their foes
   719	* Obelisks infusing the villain with power until they're destroyed
   720	* Levers that physically or magically transform parts of the battlefield
   721	* Catapults that can hurl allies to the far side of the fight
   722	* Cranes lifting heavy objects that can be dropped onto foes
   723	* Fiery cauldrons or braziers ready to tip over
   724	* Deep wells into which enemies can be dumped
   725	
   726	### Cover
   727	
   728	Shattered pillars, crumbling statues, destroyed furniture, fallen trees, and other forms of cover can help break up the otherwise open terrain of a big battleground. When you drop in these elements of cover, be sure that the players understand the advantages of hiding behind them.
   729	
   730	For bonus points, tie the history of the location and other secrets and clues to these elements of cover. It's not just a statue—it's a statue of the forgotten god Gan, lost in history and now seeking just one follower to pull their spark of divinity from the edges of infinite darkness.
   731	
   732	### Difficult or Fantastic Terrain
   733	
   734	Different areas of a location might have some sort of terrain feature that can impact the fight. Difficult terrain is the easiest option, making it challenging but not impossible to take certain routes across the battlefield. But other areas of interesting terrain can also shake up a physical encounter. Icy floors where the characters might slip don't work well as a zone-wide effect. But they can be great in specific areas, forcing the characters to avoid those areas as they move.
   735	
   736	Any of the following terrain features can make a big battle location more interesting:
   737	
   738	* A crumbling bridge over a deep crevasse
   739	* Spikes of sharp glass that cut creatures when they fall or are forced to move through them
   740	* Jets of flame that randomly erupt
   741	* Swampy land that belches forth poisonous gas when crossed
   742	* Oiled surfaces that cause creatures to slide across them uncontrollably.
   743	* Electrified floors that deal damage to creatures at the start of each turn
   744	* An area filled with antigravity magic that causes creatures to fall to the ceiling
   745	* An ethereal rift where creatures become invisible and insubstantial
   746	* Pockets of shadow where characters have their life energy drained away
   747	* An area of antilife magic where living creatures gain vulnerability to necrotic damage
   748	
   749	### A Goal
   750	
   751	Finally, think about what objective an encounter might have beyond simply taking out all the enemies. What might the characters do to "complete" the encounter? The following sorts of goals work well in a big set-piece encounter:
   752	
   753	* Stop a ritual before cultists summon a demon.
   754	* Recover an artifact and escape with it.
   755	* Kill the boss, but don't worry about their minions.
   756	* Activate a gateway and escape through it.
   757	* Recover a prisoner.
   758	* Steal secret plans.
   759	* Destroy a powerful monument.
   760	* Activate the four altars around a temple site.
   761	* Close a magical gateway and prevent the villain's escape.
   762	* Destroy a doomsday device before it blows up the multiverse.
   763	

