    98	| `source/wizard-spell-list.txt` | Complete Wizard Spell List | 79-82 | `8400870a0b7a789fc9f8cf94ea5e9faed9c1682c9bfa9bcc9fd4ee748a713926` |
    99	| `source/spell-descriptions.txt` | Complete Spell Descriptions section, enumerating 339 unique spell headings | 107-175 | `81c213de67213734b27d65c791b18686770ed3404dd0443d346ec72290057829` |
   100	| `source/unarmored-defense.txt` | Unarmored Defense — the Barbarian and Monk level-1 features, whose second ability AND shield clause differ (D75) | 29, 50 | `7e225c919bd6c225c106352d2292560ddf1791711b458dd342581896aba56af5` |
   101	
   102	### Why there is a checksum PER EXTRACT, and not only for the PDF
   103	
   104	Because the PDF's checksum did not catch the thing that broke.
   105	
   106	`source/species-descriptions.txt` was committed **truncated**. It began thirteen
   107	lines late — at the `Species Descriptions` heading rather than at the top of
   108	printed page 84 — and those thirteen lines carry the right-hand column's
   109	continuation of Dragonborn. Two whole traits were absent, `Darkvision` and
   110	`Draconic Flight`, so the file said Dragonborn has three traits where the source
   111	prints five. The file's own header claimed it existed to stop exactly that kind
   112	of undercount.
   113	
   114	The PDF checksum above matched throughout, and always would have: it says the
   115	bytes we sliced FROM are right, and says nothing about the range someone sliced
   116	OUT of them. A checksum per derived file is the only one that could have caught
   117	it, so there is now one per file and
   118	`tests/unit/rules/srd-extract-provenance.test.ts` fails if any of them drifts.
   119	
   120	The table and the directory must MATCH, in both directions. A file with no row
   121	has no provenance, and a row with no file is a claim about bytes that are not
   122	there; the same test asserts set equality. Adding an extract therefore means
   123	adding its row, which is the whole burden.
   124	
   125	### `background-descriptions.txt` is superseded by `backgrounds.txt`
   126	
   127	Two extracts of the same four backgrounds existed briefly, and this is the
   128	record of which one survives so a merge does not quietly keep both.
   129	
   130	`source/background-descriptions.txt` was page 82 sliced at column 59.
   131	`source/backgrounds.txt` is the whole of printed page 83. **Both agree on all
   132	four backgrounds**, so nothing derived from either was wrong — but the sliced
   133	file carries a slicing artifact the full-page one does not:
   134	
   135	```
   136	r-     Tool Proficiency: Calligrapher’s Supplies
   137	```
   138	
   139	That leading `r-` is the LEFT column's hyphenated `Calligrapher-` bleeding
   140	across the gutter into the right column's value. It is the exact failure the
   141	"slice by character, at the real column boundary" warning was written about,
   142	surviving in a committed file.
   143	
   144	So the full-page extract is kept and the sliced one is deleted. If a merge
   145	reintroduces `background-descriptions.txt`, `srd-extract-provenance.test.ts`
   146	fails by name until it is removed again — that is what the directory
   147	enumeration above is for.
   148	
   149	## What these extracts settle
   150	
   151	**D1b parked an open question** — whether a character's weapon-mastery count is
   152	derivable from the class data this app already stores, or needs new content. It
   153	needs new content, and the content has an unusual shape:
   154	
   155	- **Barbarian and Fighter** carry a Weapon Mastery *column* in their class
   156	  tables, so the count rises with level (Fighter 3 at levels 1-3, 4 at 4-9,
   157	  5 at 10-15, 6 at 16-20).
   158	- **Paladin, Ranger and Rogue** have no such column. Their count is a flat two,
   159	  stated in the level-1 feature text rather than in a table.
   160	
   161	So it is neither a constant nor a single progression column — it is per class,
   162	and only two of the five classes scale it. Anything modelling this must handle
   163	both shapes. Nothing in the app's current `class_progressions` carries it,
   164	because that table models spellcasting only (see F4).
   165	
   166	Weapon mastery is a 2024-rules concept: every weapon has exactly one mastery
   167	property, and a character unlocks it only through a feature such as Weapon
   168	Mastery. The property is attached to the weapon; the *permission* is attached
   169	to the character.
   170	
   171	## The three extracts added for the sheet core, and why
   172	
   173	`skills-table.txt`, `sheet-math.txt` and `multiclassing.txt` were added because
   174	a review of what the sheet needs found that **six of its numbers had no source
   175	in this directory at all**: the skill-to-ability mapping, the level-1 and
   176	per-level Hit Point arithmetic, unarmoured Armor Class, Initiative, Passive
   177	Perception, and the multiclass rules for Proficiency Bonus and proficiencies.
   178	
   179	Every one of those was recallable from memory and none of it was written down,
   180	which is the exact failure F6 exists to prevent. Writing the code first and the
   181	provenance later would have produced values that look right and cannot be
   182	checked. The extraction cost an hour and removed the whole fabrication surface.
   183	
   184	Two measured facts worth recording, because both contradict something that was
   185	previously believed here:
   186	
   187	- **The Skills table has 18 rows and `Performance` is one of them.** The twelve
   188	  class Core Traits tables between them name only 17 skills — `Performance`
   189	  appears in no class's list. A skills vocabulary "closed on evidence" the way
   190	  `weaponMasteryProperties` is would have been 17 skills and silently wrong.
   191	  The Skills table is the source that closes it, not the class lists.
   192	- **The Extra Attack extract could not be attributed to classes as first
   193	  written.** Its seven rows carried no class names, so deciding which class each
   194	  belonged to would have come from memory. The section has been re-extracted
   195	  with each class's Features table title and column headers above its own rows.
   196	
   197	## The complete text, committed
   198	
   199	`docs/srd/full/srd-5.2.1.txt` is the entire `pdftotext -layout` output of the
   200	document above after its notice header, plus the bounded Telekinesis repair
   201	recorded above. It was committed 2026-07-30 at the owner's direction ("we will
   202	need it all"). The PDF was re-fetched that day and its SHA-256 matched this
   203	file's table exactly before conversion. Committed file SHA-256:
   204	`d2425fa863247509c9af77cd4856e254a9ad4216661b948fc99daa67db69c918`
   205	(2,147,059 bytes; re-pinned 2026-08-17 for the Telekinesis repair and the
   206	header's corresponding provenance correction).
   207	
   208	Two-column pages interleave their columns on each line in that file, so the
   209	extracts in `source/` remain the readable, column-sliced references and every
   210	future slice should still land there with a row in the table above. What the
   211	full file changes is provenance: a new slice is now checked against a
   212	COMMITTED source, not a download.
   213	
   214	## Attribution
   215	
   216	`ATTRIBUTION.md` in this directory governs. Every file in `source/` carries the
   217	required notice verbatim at the top of the file, because that notice must
   218	travel with the content and a directory-level licence file does not survive a
   219	copied file.
   220	

