   680	   Reviewers stop earning Highs for in-process self-attacks; the arms race ends.
   681	
   682	## D249 — OWNER: UI is designed by Claude first, through a four-stage pipeline (2026-08-14)
   683	
   684	Ruling on how UI work is produced, overriding the general "codex implements"
   685	default for UI only:
   686	
   687	1. **Claude design skills first.** The official `frontend-design` skill is
   688	   already installed and is the starting point, not an afterthought.
   689	2. **Fable plans** the UI, from several perspectives.
   690	3. **Opus creates** it.
   691	4. **Fable reviews** it, again from several perspectives.
   692	5. **codex `-p sol` is a second reviewer**, explicitly for diversity — a
   693	   different model family sees different failures.
   694	
   695	This is a standing authorization for Fable subagents on UI work specifically,
   696	which otherwise require asking first. It does not extend to non-UI work, where
   697	codex remains the implementer. Claude sonnet remains prohibited everywhere.
   698	
   699	## D248 — OWNER: fan out into worktrees as far as possible (2026-08-14)
   700	
   701	Asked what to do after the R3 and simulation merges. Ruled: **fan out into
   702	worktrees as much as possible**, each worktree proving all tests pass before it
   703	tries to merge back, each with its own database, using workflows where the work
   704	suits them.
   705	
   706	**Reconciliation with the one-suite-at-a-time amendment**, which this ruling
   707	does not repeal but does constrain: lanes fan out without limit and run
   708	FOCUSED tests themselves; separate databases remove the shared-fixture
   709	collisions; the supervisor still runs each full suite, and runs them SERIALLY
   710	on a quiet box. Parallelism therefore lives in the work, and serialization
   711	lives only in the timing-sensitive gate. A lane that self-gates a full suite
   712	while another does the same produces false timeouts, and that failure has
   713	already been observed twice.
   714	
   715	## D247 — OWNER: a first-landing subsystem is reviewed until clean, with no round cap (2026-08-14)
   716	
   717	The three-round review cap does not apply while a subsystem is landing for the
   718	first time. Prompted by the simulation core, whose first review round found
   719	three real defects including a refusal that silently became zero. The reasoning
   720	that governs future cases: early defects set the shape everything later builds
   721	on, so the code that most deserves review attention is exactly the code a round
   722	cap would cut short.
   723	
   724	## D246 — OWNER: the simulator's first shippable slice covers attacks, saves and resources (2026-08-14)
   725	
   726	Not the narrow weapon-attack-only slice. The first flag-gated release must
   727	carry ordinary attacks, saving-throw damage effects, AND the rounds /
   728	short-rest / long-rest resource budget — because settings that do not change
   729	the result are decoration, and a simulator that cannot model a save is not one
   730	an advanced user can trust.
   731	
   732	Cost accepted: longer to reach honestly, and saving-throw evidence is precisely
   733	where the first review round found a defect.
   734	
   735	## D245 — OWNER: a number ships only when every gap is provably zero (2026-08-14)
   736	
   737	When a character carries a mechanic the simulator cannot source, the result
   738	shows a number ONLY if every excluded mechanic is proved damage-neutral for the
   739	chosen routine. Otherwise it refuses and names the blockers. Rejected both the
   740	partial-number-with-caveats option and whole-result refusal.
   741	
   742	This is the strictest honest option and it creates real work: each exclusion
   743	needs a per-mechanic proof of neutrality, not an assumption. It also settles
   744	the review finding that an unsourced save-success clause could be labelled
   745	`none` and contribute zero — under this ruling, zero and unknown are different
   746	values and the second one is not printable.
